WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.650
- This week, I've been sharing with you

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.690
some authors whose words have helped me

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.240
get through tough times, like 2020.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.590
These writers offer the kind
of insight we can all use

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.100
as we start 2021 on the right foot.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.280
So one of those is fellow
Texan, Brene Brown,

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.416
who's published five New
York Times bestsellers.

00:00:19.416 --> 00:00:22.900
I mean, one topic she
brilliantly tackles is self-care.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:25.850
She writes, "Daring to set
boundaries is about having

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.000
the courage to love ourselves

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.920
even when we risk disappointing others."

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.330
Have you ever gone to church,

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:33.740
like you hadn't been in awhile,

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.661
then you show up and you're
like, oh, snap, that was for me?

00:00:37.661 --> 00:00:40.550
You're like, God is definitely
talking to me right now.

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.200
Yeah, that's like that quote.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.400
Yeah, all right.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.660
Well that powerful concept doesn't just

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:46.890
resonate big with me.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.920
We actually have someone with us who says

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:50.530
it summarizes her 2020.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:52.941
Meet Logan, everybody.

00:00:52.941 --> 00:00:55.333
(applause and cheers)
What's up, Logan?

00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.220
- Hi, Kelly, how are you?

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.573
- I'm not as good as your lips right now.

00:00:58.573 --> 00:01:00.938
I love that color, it's so pretty.

00:01:00.938 --> 00:01:02.790
- Thank you, Kelly.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.580
- Oh yeah, you look so cute.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.050
- Thank you.
- Well, you say that quote

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.330
helped you in the past, in this past year

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:09.430
more than ever, right?

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:10.290
- Definitely.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.960
I mean, for years I was
available for everyone,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.380
family, friends, coworkers,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.820
whatever they needed to talk
about, I was always there.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.900
But you know, meanwhile, I
couldn't get anything done.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.500
I was missing deadlines at work.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.610
I was like, okay, something has to change.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.650
So I love Brene, I've read
"Gifts of Imperfections".

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:30.630
I've read "Daring Greatly".

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.880
And when she talked about
boundaries in an interview

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.470
I was like, okay, this is it.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.640
She said, "Knowing what's
okay and what's not okay,

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.100
that's how you implement your boundaries."

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.100
- Yeah, and it's a really
hard thing to develop

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.650
if you are a people pleaser.

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.130
It is like, I mean, I've
heard from other people,

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.377
I'm great at it.

00:01:50.377 --> 00:01:52.018
(laughter)

00:01:52.018 --> 00:01:54.760
So what was it hard to
set those boundaries?

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.420
'Cause I always feel mean,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.100
even though you're not being mean,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.401
but you feel like you're
letting someone down.

00:02:01.401 --> 00:02:03.700
Was it hard for you to really start

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:06.601
taking the advice and actually living it?

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:09.820
- For the people who are not
gonna respect our boundaries,

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.230
they're gonna fall off anyway.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.460
And the people that you care
about, you can just say,

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:15.980
hey, you know, this is
what I told my family.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.770
I said, "I love you, but
from 5:00 PM to 9:00 PM

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:20.030
I'm gonna be working on something.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.530
So my phone is gonna
be on, do not disturb."

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:25.839
And if there's any missed
calls or any missed messages,

00:02:25.839 --> 00:02:29.370
if there was an emergency,
I reply to the message.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.859
And then I had to implement
my own personal boundary

00:02:31.859 --> 00:02:34.290
by not replying, even if I really want to,

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.441
'cause what I really
learned is people only

00:02:36.441 --> 00:02:37.820
listen to your actions.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.839
They don't listen to your words.

00:02:39.839 --> 00:02:40.839
- Ugh, fine.

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:44.453
(laughter)
It's so hard.

00:02:46.220 --> 00:02:47.510
Great news, everybody.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:50.540
Brene Brown is actually here
with practical words of wisdom

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:53.561
we can all use to start off 2021 right.

00:02:53.561 --> 00:02:56.400
I am so excited to get to talk to her.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:57.460
What's up everybody?

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.997
I'm back with Tim Allen and Mary Mouser,

00:02:59.997 --> 00:03:01.649
give it up, give it up!

00:03:01.649 --> 00:03:02.610
(applause and cheers)

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:04.340
All right, y'all, well this
week we are celebrating

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.220
the start of 2021 and
maybe more importantly,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:09.200
the end of 2020.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.187
We are saying, "Well
that sucked, moving on."

00:03:12.187 --> 00:03:14.747
(dramatic music)
- Well that sucked.

00:03:14.747 --> 00:03:16.347
♪ Moving on ♪

00:03:16.347 --> 00:03:20.130
- I love it, every time, I love it.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.688
My next guest is a five time, five time

00:03:22.688 --> 00:03:26.160
New York Times bestseller whose
words have inspired millions

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.760
of people far and wide, including myself.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.477
In fact, I was reading her book,

00:03:30.477 --> 00:03:32.200
"The Gifts of Imperfection" this summer

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.560
and tweeted this at one
o'clock in the morning.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:36.320
No joke, I could not put the book down

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:38.670
and I said, "Up late
reading some Brene Brown

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.630
and she is just servin' up
some slap you in the face

00:03:41.630 --> 00:03:44.550
honesty in regard to shame and boundaries.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.030
Good Lord, it's like get
out of my head lady."

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:49.220
But I'm absolutely thrilled
she's joining us today.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.486
Please, everyone, say hi to Brene Brown!

00:03:52.486 --> 00:03:54.806
(applause and cheers)

00:03:54.806 --> 00:03:56.128
What's up girl?

00:03:56.128 --> 00:03:57.627
- Hi!

00:03:57.627 --> 00:03:59.000
- Hello!

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:01.328
Meet Mary and meet Tim.

00:04:01.328 --> 00:04:03.370
- Hi!
- Hi, Mary, hi, Tim.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:07.490
- All right, well hey,
Brene, I mean, 2020 was hard

00:04:07.490 --> 00:04:09.731
on all of us, it doesn't
matter who you are.

00:04:09.731 --> 00:04:11.920
Relatively speaking in everyone's life,

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.480
everyone kind of had a hard 2020

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.950
and just hard time navigating it.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.947
What was your biggest challenge?

00:04:16.947 --> 00:04:18.560
I wanna ask this question to people

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.650
I think that they've got
all their stuff figured out,

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:22.867
for lack of a better word.

00:04:22.867 --> 00:04:25.790
You know, what was your challenge?

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.810
- I'm definitely the wrong
person to be talking to

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.300
if you're looking for someone who's got

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.597
their stuff figured out.

00:04:31.666 --> 00:04:36.160
Yeah, for me, I have to
say it was probably one

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.893
of the hardest seasons
of my 30 year marriage.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:45.870
I think parenting was,
every decision we made

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.870
was like a NASA DEFCON 5
cost benefit risk analysis.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.610
It was hard.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.610
And one of the things that
I don't think we understood

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:01.022
completely is that we're
not wired neurobiologically

00:05:01.022 --> 00:05:04.567
for a crisis that lasts that long.

00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:09.567
We have enough adrenaline, we
have enough kind of reserve

00:05:10.104 --> 00:05:14.626
to push through most of
the crises that we face.

00:05:14.626 --> 00:05:17.420
I'm in Houston, so you know, hurricanes,

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.884
but we're not wired for a nine month.

00:05:19.884 --> 00:05:24.050
We're depleted, we're weary, we wear out.

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.680
And then we have to consciously be aware

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:31.680
of our choices or we turn
into not our best selves.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.807
- Amen.
- And we saw a lot of that,

00:05:34.807 --> 00:05:35.640
I think, in 2020.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.150
- Yeah, no, amen.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:40.820
But earlier Logan told
us Brene's definition

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:44.440
of boundaries changed
her life and boundaries,

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.548
I'm gonna be honest with
you, are not my forte.

00:05:47.548 --> 00:05:49.379
(laughter)

00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:53.710
Brene, how is giving less of
your time considered generous?

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:57.346
'Cause how do I sell that
to people, is my question?

00:05:57.346 --> 00:05:59.870
(laughter)

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:02.760
- Okay, so this is the
weirdest part of our research.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.730
So early on, maybe 10 years in,

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.350
I had this stack of data and I was like,

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.270
these are the most compassionate people

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:10.103
I've ever come across.

00:06:10.103 --> 00:06:12.420
I mean, we were interviewing like monks.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:14.700
We were interviewing really
compassionate people.

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:16.230
And so we asked ourselves,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:18.380
what do these folks have in common?

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.870
And I mean, we're talking
about close to 500,000

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.640
pieces of data over 20 years now.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:25.447
And we're like, and I thought
the answer was gonna be

00:06:25.447 --> 00:06:27.990
spirituality, like compassionate people

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:29.950
have spirituality in common,

00:06:29.950 --> 00:06:30.810
but I was wrong.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.450
The answer is the most
compassionate people

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.030
that we have interviewed and studied

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:38.667
in the last 20 years
are the most boundaried.

00:06:38.667 --> 00:06:42.410
And so what I've learned
is that boundaries are not,

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:44.800
you know, as someone who studies
vulnerability, I would say

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.068
boundaries are not a wall
or a moat around your heart.

00:06:49.068 --> 00:06:51.700
They are the path to self-respect.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.690
Boundaries are saying
that I choose self-love

00:06:55.690 --> 00:06:58.890
and self-respect over what you think of me

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.880
or the possibility of disappointing you

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:04.470
and how this ties to
generosity, and the research

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.190
is really interesting
and here's the thing,

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:10.990
we're not generous and kind to people

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:12.910
who are walking all over us.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:14.604
- Yeah, I mean, Tim,

00:07:14.604 --> 00:07:16.210
are you good at boundaries?

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:17.540
I've been seeing him shake his head.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:18.740
- It depends.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:22.710
And here's my problem,
is I can do it in work,

00:07:22.710 --> 00:07:24.210
I can set boundaries immediately.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.040
Comes off as being kind of angry sometimes

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.230
when I just, that's it,

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:30.760
I don't wanna do this anymore.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.220
I don't want this.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.460
When I get around my mother, I'm finally

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:37.884
to that point, I no longer,
this is not okay with me.

00:07:37.884 --> 00:07:39.190
I love you, I'm your son.

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:43.250
I'll do whatever, but I'm not a footstool,

00:07:43.250 --> 00:07:44.840
or whatever I came up with.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.050
There are people in my
life that I'm unable,

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.524
I'm virtually powerless.

00:07:49.524 --> 00:07:51.770
I get taken advantage of,
there's no boundaries.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:54.240
I walk away in my world, I get resentful.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.350
- I was gonna say, yeah.
- That's poison to me.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.650
- And then resentment sets in, me too!

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.908
And then I'm mad at myself.

00:08:00.908 --> 00:08:04.220
- I said to my kid, she
goes, "You're doing it now."

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.130
And I said, "You know what?

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:08.700
There's certain kids like a dirty diaper

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:12.220
because it's smelly and warm,
but at least it's theirs."

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:16.120
And in my case, sometimes I
like sitting in resentment.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.990
There's something about it that I like.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.070
That's to be looked at.

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.630
At I look at that sometimes,
I let people take advantage

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:24.319
of me and underneath I'm a terrorist.

00:08:24.319 --> 00:08:27.540
I'll show them, but it won't be now,

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:29.021
it might be tomorrow.

00:08:29.021 --> 00:08:31.409
(laughter)

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:32.833
- You're like Dr. Evil.
- Make that person pay

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:35.866
for leaving their dog poo in my yard.

00:08:35.866 --> 00:08:37.700
- I was gonna say, I just
get passive aggressive.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.246
- No, here's the thing.

00:08:39.246 --> 00:08:42.699
I'm like you too, but I don't
like living in resentment.

00:08:42.699 --> 00:08:44.890
But here's the thing, I think that also

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:49.570
you can't really like, I
feel in my own, whatever,

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.480
path, journey, LA word I wanna use,

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:56.210
whatever, my own thing
is that you can't really

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.570
resent the person if you're
allowing it to happen.

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:00.780
Like you can't.
- Exactly.

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.940
- And so that's the thing,
it's kinda your fault.

00:09:02.940 --> 00:09:04.270
It's my fault, you know?

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.160
And that's the hard thing though,

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.840
but then it comes into shame
and then it comes into guilt.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.410
And then it's just like, Jesus,

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:12.574
there's not enough time in this show.

00:09:12.574 --> 00:09:15.970
(laughter)
So there's a lot

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:16.870
that goes into it.

00:09:17.764 --> 00:09:20.730
It's such a bigger conversation,
but I really do implore

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:23.490
that everybody, it's the 10th anniversary

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:26.040
of Brene's book, "The
Gifts of Imperfection".

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.450
It's the one I tweeted about.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.470
She's got a ton of books you can get.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:31.130
This is the one that really just hit home

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:32.983
for me whenever I really needed it.

00:09:34.328 --> 00:09:35.510
But I highly recommend you read it

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.700
and implement it into your lives.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.237
Be sure to check out her
podcast, "Unlocking Us".

